wn Board</h3><p>In the red zone MIN throw it (61% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Justin Jefferson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A middling 44 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>GB \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>GB are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>36%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p><strong>Josh Jacobs</strong> (12 carries, 48 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Christian Watson</strong> projects for <strong>5 targets and 52 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tucker Kraft</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 51 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>MIN \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>MIN are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Aaron Jones</strong> (14 carries, 65 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Justin Jefferson</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 71 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>T.J. Hockenson</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 32 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 2-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"GB","col":"#FFD84D","players":[{"name":"Jordan Love","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":241.77,"book":239.5,"over_th":227.18,"under_th":251.73,"over_am":"\u2212114","under_am":"\u2212114","pover":50,"call":"none","ev":-5.7,"edge":2.3,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.79,"book":1.5,"over_th":1.06,"under_th":1.45,"over_am":"\u2212100","under_am":"\u2212129","pover":44,"call":"none","ev":0.1,"edge":0.3,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":9.9,"fair":"10/1","book":"8/1","value":false},"d":{"pass_yards":{"m":241.77,"q":[111.02,180.11,238.95,302.86,369.81],"line":239.5,"od":1.88,"ud":1.88},"pass_td":{"m":1.79,"q":[0.28,0.28,1.25,2.22,3.19],"line":1.5,"od":2.0,"ud":1.78},"rush_yards":{"m":12.27,"q":[0.0,2.1,9.1,18.72,30.1],"line":10.5,"od":1.87,"ud":1.88},"rush_att":{"m":2.95,"q":[0.0,0.83,3.16,4.32,6.65],"line":2.5,"od":1.75,"ud":2.05}}},{"name":"Josh Jacobs","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Ru